// 
// This is a simple example of a contract test. Tests are designed to check that methods behave correctly in a number of different situations, ensuring that correct output and state are produced under different inputs. Contract tests are particularly useful when testing libraries or components, since they can test that a class/method behaves correctly when used in a broad range of contexts.
// 
// Let's suppose that you have written some code and later want to assure its correct behavior. You can write a test for it, which will use some known input parameters and check the output in comparison to the expected result. Pex allows to perform this process by generating a number of different inputs and feeding them for testing methods.
// 
// The Pex framework allows you to generate test data (fuzzing). When the code under test is not predictable (is not clear how it works), especially when there're different branches or when there're many possible actions), it could crash, or behave unexpectedly. Pex can generate many different inputs which can be used in your tests to check the robustness of your code. Also, Pex can generate tests that exercise the boundaries and the code under test.
// 
// The Pex plugin for MSBuild can be used to run Pex tests automatically along with your other tests. Pex tests can be run on check-ins and continuously on the build server to check for potential new issues. Also, Pex tests can be used to generate new methods that can be used in the production code.
// 
// Pex helps you produce faster, more reliable and more maintainable code. It will save you time investigating complex bugs and is free for OSS. Pex requires .NET Framework 4.5 and later.
